            <ArticleTitle>Autologous method for ex vivo expansion of human limbal epithelial progenitor cells based on plasma rich in growth factors technology.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>248-256</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1542-0124(16)30183-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jtos.2017.01.003</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Develop an autologous culture method for ex vivo expansion of human limbal epithelial progenitor cells (LEPCs) using Plasma Rich in Growth Factors (PRGF) as a growth supplement and as a scaffold for the culture of LEPCs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">LEPCs were cultivated in different media supplemented with 10% fetal bovine serum (FBS) or 10% PRGF. The outgrowths, total number of cells, colony forming efficiency (CFE), morphology and immunocytochemistry against p63- α and cytokeratins 3 and 12 (CK3-CK12) were analyzed. PRGF was also used to elaborate a fibrin membrane. The effects of the scaffold on the preservation of stemness and the phenotypic characterization of LEPCs were investigated through analysis of CK3-CK12, ABCG-2 and p63.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">LEPCs cultivated with PRGF showed a significantly higher growth area than FBS cultures. Moreover, the number of cells were also higher in PRGF than FBS, while displaying a better morphology overall. CFE was found to be also higher in PRGF groups compared to FBS, and the p63-α expression also differed between groups. LEPCs cultivated on PRGF membranes appeared as a confluent monolayer of cells and still retained p63 and ABCG-2 expression, being negative for CK3-CK12.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">PRGF can be used in corneal tissue engineering, supplementing the culture media, even in a basal media without any other additives, as well as providing a scaffold for the culture.</AbstractText>
